<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/ecology-populations-communities">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Biology. It needs zero prior knowledge. The
    only equipment it asks for is a patch of grass and the patience to watch
    it — because every big idea in this lesson can be seen there: who eats
    whom, where the food's energy came from, and why the grass never takes
    over the whole planet.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace the flow of energy from sunlight through a food chain, and say why it only ever moves one way;</li>
    <li>use the words producer, consumer and decomposer correctly;</li>
    <li>draw a simple food chain and read a food web;</li>
    <li>sketch the carbon and nitrogen cycles, and say what returns each one;</li>
    <li>explain why populations grow, slow down and level off.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a lawn</h2>
  <p>
    Look at any bit of green outdoors — a lawn, a roadside verge, a park.
    Grass grows there. Rabbits or insects eat the grass. Birds eat the
    insects. A cat watches the birds. Underneath it all, worms and microbes
    turn everything that dies back into soil. You have just seen the whole
    subject of <strong>ecology</strong> — the study of how living things
    interact with each other and with their surroundings. An
    <strong>ecosystem</strong> is one such patch of interacting life plus its
    physical setting.
  </p>
  <p>
    Strip away the variety of species and four questions remain — and they
    are the four topics of this lesson. Where does the
    <strong>energy</strong> that runs an ecosystem come from, and where does
    it go? Who eats whom — the <strong>food chains and webs</strong> that map
    feeding relationships? How do <strong>nutrients</strong> come back after
    being locked into bodies? And what makes any one
    <strong>population</strong> — a group of the same species living in one
    place — grow, crash or hover?
  </p>
  <p>
    The subject rewards one habit above all: following quantities rather
    than names. Sunlight becomes grass, grass becomes rabbit, rabbit becomes
    cat, and everything eventually becomes soil again. The arithmetic of
    those flows explains why big fierce animals are rare, and why nothing in
    an ecosystem is ever really thrown away.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ecology studies who eats whom and how matter
    and energy move. Next: the energy itself — where it enters, and why it
    leaks away.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy flow: the one-way street</h2>
  <p>
    Energy enters the living world almost entirely through
    <strong>producers</strong> — plants, algae and some bacteria that
    photosynthesise, turning sunlight into the chemical energy of sugar.
    Everything else is a <strong>consumer</strong>, eating that stored energy
    second-hand: <strong>herbivores</strong> (plant-eaters) eat producers,
    <strong>carnivores</strong> (meat-eaters) eat herbivores, and so on.
    Each feeding step is called a <strong>trophic level</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    At every step, energy leaks away in forms life cannot re-eat: heat lost
    during respiration (living things cannot harvest heat back into useful
    work), body parts that are never swallowed, waste that is never
    absorbed. The rule of thumb is stark: <strong>only about 10% of the
    energy at one trophic level makes it to the next</strong> (measured
    transfers run roughly 5–20%). So a field carrying 1,000 kg of grass can
    support about 100 kg of herbivore — and about 10 kg of carnivore.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequences are arithmetic, not metaphor. Food chains rarely exceed
    four or five links, because there is simply nothing left to eat the
    lion-eater. Top predators are always rare and always vulnerable — they
    need the most territory per individual. And eating lower on the food
    chain supports more people per hectare of planet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Completing the circuit are the <strong>decomposers</strong> — bacteria
    and fungi — which break down the dead and the dung. Crucially, they do
    not add a trophic level of their own: their job is releasing locked-up
    nutrients for the producers to re-use. Every ecosystem is thus two
    networks in one: energy flowing one way from sun to heat, and matter
    cycling endlessly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "matter and energy both cycle through ecosystems."
    Only matter cycles. Energy enters once as sunlight, passes up the chain losing heat
    at every link, and leaves for space as infrared radiation — a one-way flow, which is
    why ecosystems need a continuous power supply and why the sun matters. Nutrients
    loop; energy does not, and confusing the two makes decomposers look like a power
    source rather than the recycling crew they are.
  </p>

  <h2>Food chains and food webs</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>food chain</strong> is one feeding pathway written in a line:
    grass &rarr; wildebeest &rarr; lion. The arrows point the way the energy
    goes — always from the eaten to the eater. Real ecosystems are too
    tangled for single chains, so ecologists draw <strong>food
    webs</strong>: every species linked to everything it eats or is eaten
    by. Webs show three things chains cannot.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Redundancy:</strong> most predators eat several prey, so one collapse rarely starves a specialist — until you remove something connected enough.</li>
    <li><strong>Trophic position is a role, not a species:</strong> a fox eats berries and rabbits, occupying two levels at once; a human at a banquet spans three.</li>
    <li><strong>Hierarchy can be measured:</strong> ecological pyramids stack the levels as bars — a pyramid of <em>numbers</em> (count the organisms), of <em>biomass</em> (weigh the living tissue) and, the only honest one, of <em>energy</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Numbers and biomass pyramids occasionally invert — one oak tree feeding
    thousands of aphids — but an energy pyramid never does. You cannot
    support more kilojoules of consumer than the producer level makes, any
    more than a salary can be spent twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run the arithmetic on one hectare and the pyramid becomes a budget.
    Suppose the vegetation stores the equivalent of 100 million kilojoules a
    year. At the 10% rule of thumb, herbivores can harvest about 10 million,
    primary carnivores about a million, and a top predator perhaps a hundred
    thousand — a few person-days of food energy from an entire hectare. That
    is the quantitative meaning of "big fierce animals are rare". Feed the
    same hectare's production to people directly, and the ledger improves
    enormously: a hectare of grain feeds far more humans than a hectare of
    grain-fed beef. The choice is arithmetic, not taste.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "plants are just the background; the real action is
    animals." The reverse is true energetically: producers are the <em>only</em> entry
    point of energy, typically ~1–2% of incident sunlight is captured, and every animal
    calorie is second-hand plant. Ecosystems are plant-driven from the bottom up.
  </p>

  <h2>Nutrient cycles: matter's round trips</h2>
  <p>
    Energy is a one-way river; matter is a closed loop. The same carbon and
    nitrogen atoms have cycled through living things for billions of years.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the <strong>carbon cycle</strong>, photosynthesis pulls CO<sub>2</sub>
    from the air into sugar; respiration by producers, consumers and
    decomposers pushes it back. Burning fossil fuels releases carbon buried
    for hundreds of millions of years — which is why the atmosphere's carbon
    dioxide has climbed from about 280 ppm (parts per million,
    pre-industrial) to over 420 ppm today. The carbon cycle now runs with an
    extra faucet open.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>water cycle</strong> moves the same water endlessly:
    evaporation from oceans and leaves, then condensation into cloud, then
    precipitation back down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>nitrogen cycle</strong> is the one with the most biology in
    it. Air is 78% nitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>), but that triple-bonded molecule
    is unusable until <em>nitrogen-fixing bacteria</em> — free-living, or
    housed in the root nodules of legumes such as beans and clover — convert
    it to ammonium and nitrates that plants can absorb. Other bacteria return
    nitrogen to the air. Farming bends this cycle heavily: the Haber process
    now fixes more nitrogen industrially than all natural land processes
    combined, and the excess runs off as the algal-bloom dead zones
    downstream of fertilised fields.
  </p>
  <p>
    The general lesson of all nutrient cycles: <strong>decomposers are not
    an afterthought but the return leg</strong>. An ecosystem that cannot
    recycle is a mine, not a home.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> energy flows one way and leaks at every meal;
    matter cycles — carbon and nitrogen loop through air, life and soil,
    with decomposers running the return leg. Next: what makes any one
    population rise or fall.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="exponential promise, logistic reality — the S-curve is ecology's first law" widget="plotter" config={ecoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the two curves rise together at first —
    early on, every population grows as if resources were unlimited. Then
    the green S-curve bends away and flattens while the red exponential
    keeps climbing straight off the top of the graph. The ceiling the green
    curve settles towards is the carrying capacity, and its steepest stretch
    sits near half of that — the middle of the S.
  </p>

  <h2>Populations: growth, limits and cycles</h2>
  <p>
    A population grows, ideally, exponentially: every individual adds
    offspring, so growth is proportional to the number already there — the
    red curve in the graph above. Given unlimited resources, that is what
    any population does; a bacterium doubling every 20 minutes would weigh
    as much as the Earth within two days. Real environments bite back. Food,
    space, light, water, predation, disease — the <strong>limiting
    factors</strong> — slow the growth, and it levels off at the
    <strong>carrying capacity</strong> of the environment: the green
    S-shaped curve. The interesting part is the middle of the S, where
    growth is fastest — which is why fisheries can be harvested hardest at
    about half the carrying capacity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Overshoot the limit and reality demonstrates itself. On St Matthew
    Island, 29 reindeer were released in 1944. They hit over 6,000 by 1963 —
    and then the lichen they grazed ran out, leaving only tens of survivors
    by 1966. A population that crashes from overshoot is this lesson's
    cautionary worked example.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "carrying capacity is a fixed number for a habitat."
    K is a moving target set by current conditions: overgrazed rangeland supports fewer
    animals next season (the St Matthew reindeer crashed their own K), a wet year raises
    it, a drought halves it, and human technology has raised humanity's K repeatedly.
    Populations do not sit gently under a fixed ceiling; they stand on a floor that
    shifts under their feet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Populations also <strong>couple</strong> to each other. The classic
    dataset is a century of Hudson Bay fur records: lynx and snowshoe hare
    numbers rise and fall together, roughly every decade, with the lynx peak
    <em>lagging</em> the hare peak by a year or two. More hares feed more
    lynx; more lynx kill more hares; fewer hares starve the lynx; fewer lynx
    free the hares. It is a predator–prey oscillator built from nothing but
    birth and death rates. (Reality is messier than the textbook curve —
    vegetation plays a third role — but the lagged chase is real, and the
    ecologist Gause reproduced it in laboratory vessels with Paramecium and
    yeast.)
  </p>
  <p>
    Within communities, populations are organised by <strong>niches</strong>
    — a species' role and address: what it eats, when it feeds, where it
    shelters. Gause's competitive exclusion principle says two species
    cannot occupy the same niche indefinitely — the better competitor drives
    the other out — so coexisting species always differ <em>somehow</em>:
    Darwin's Galápagos finches partitioned beak sizes; warblers partition
    feeding heights in the same tree. Some members matter
    disproportionately: <strong>keystone species</strong> — sea otters
    suppressing sea urchins so kelp forests persist; wolves restructuring
    Yellowstone's rivers through elk behaviour — whose removal rewrites the
    whole community. And after a disturbance, communities rebuild in a
    predictable order called <strong>succession</strong>: bare rock, then
    lichens, then grasses, then shrubs, then woodland — each stage
    engineering the soil and shade that the next one requires.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write out a three-link food chain, and say which way the arrows point and why.</li>
    <li>Only about 10% of energy survives a feeding step. Name two places the other 90% goes.</li>
    <li>A population keeps growing past its food supply. What happens next?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Grass &rarr; rabbit &rarr; fox — arrows run
    from the eaten to the eater, because that is the direction the energy
    travels. (2) Heat lost during respiration, and parts or waste that are
    never absorbed — either answer plus egested waste is fine. (3) It
    overshoots the carrying capacity, runs down its resources, and crashes —
    as the St Matthew reindeer did.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Energy enters life only through producers and flows one way, leaking heat at every step; matter, unlike energy, cycles endlessly.</li>
    <li>Only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level reaches the next — which is why food chains are short and top predators rare.</li>
    <li>Food webs add redundancy and roles, and an energy pyramid never inverts even when pyramids of numbers or biomass can.</li>
    <li>Decomposers run the return leg of every nutrient cycle — carbon, water and nitrogen loop through air, life and soil.</li>
    <li>Populations grow exponentially in principle but level off at the carrying capacity; overshoot the limit and the population crashes.</li>
    <li>Predator and prey populations oscillate with a lag, niches partition communities, and keystone species hold those communities together.</li>
    <li>Populations are estimated, not counted: quadrats for slow or rooted organisms, mark–recapture for mobile ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Ecology runs on the rest of the course. Photosynthesis and respiration
    drive the energy flow and the carbon cycle traced here, evolution by
    natural selection shapes the adaptations behind every niche, and
    classification supplies the keys that name the species being counted.
    The human population, read here as an S-curve on its upper bend, is the
    backdrop for the environmental decisions this subject exists to inform.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative skeleton is worth knowing. The logistic curve is
    dN/dt = rN × (1 − N/K): growth is the exponential term rN, damped by the
    fraction of carrying capacity left. <strong>r and K
    strategists</strong> name the two ends of the life-history spectrum:
    r-selected species (insects, weeds, many fish) produce enormous numbers
    of cheap offspring to colonise fresh gaps; K-selected species
    (elephants, whales, humans) invest heavily in few offspring that compete
    near saturation. Most of conservation is deciding which regime a
    threatened species lives in — pandas are K-strategists in a shrinking K,
    which is the whole problem. Human population history is the S-curve read
    at planetary scale: about 1 billion by 1804 after ten millennia of slow
    growth, then the demographic transition — death rates falling before
    birth rates — carried it past 8 billion by 2022, with growth now
    decelerating as fertility falls across most of the world. We are
    visibly on the upper bend of the S.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open edges: ecology's forecasting problem is that communities are
    networks with time lags and feedback, so linear extrapolation fails —
    sudden "regime shifts" (a lake flipping from clear to turbid) are the
    field's version of a phase change. The ecosystem-services view puts the
    accounting on people's books: pollination (roughly a third of food
    crops depend on animal pollinators), soil formation and water
    purification are flows the cycles deliver for free. And the deepest open
    question is diversity's role: <em>why</em> do more diverse communities
    produce more biomass and resist invasion more stably? Decades of
    experiments (the Cedar Creek grassland plots are the canonical case) say
    they do; the mechanisms — niche complementarity, sampling effects,
    insurance across species — are still being weighed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Measuring any of this requires field method, because populations are
    never counted exhaustively. Slow or rooted organisms are sampled with
    <strong>quadrats</strong> — square frames placed many times, with the
    average scaled up to the habitat's area. If thirty quadrats of 1 m²
    average 4 daisies each, the field holds about 4 daisies per square metre
    — roughly 40 in a 10 m² patch — and the reliability comes from the
    number and spread of the throws, not the frame's precision. Mobile
    animals use <strong>mark–recapture</strong>: catch, mark and release N
    individuals; later catch n, of which m are marked. The Lincoln index
    estimates the population as N × n ÷ m (50 marked, 40 recaptured, 10 of
    them marked again gives 200 animals), on the honest assumptions that
    marking neither harms nor changes behaviour and that the population is
    closed between samples. The assumptions <em>are</em> the lesson: every
    ecology number arrives with its sampling story attached, and reading
    that story is part of reading the number.
  </p>
